# Keeps the Firebase project config out of git history.
# NOTE: this only stops git from tracking the file — it does NOT stop Netlify
# from needing it. A browser has to load this file at runtime to initialize
# Firebase, so:
#   - If you deploy by dragging the project folder into Netlify (or via
#     `netlify deploy` from this folder), it works as-is: gitignore only
#     affects git, not what's on disk, so the file still gets uploaded.
#   - If Netlify is connected to this repo on GitHub and auto-deploys on
#     push, this file will NOT reach Netlify, since it never reaches GitHub.
#     In that case, either add it directly in the Netlify UI (Site
#     configuration > Deploys > Post processing, or drag-and-drop a manual
#     deploy alongside your git-based one), or move these values into
#     Netlify's environment variables and inject them at build time instead.
firebase-config.js
